### 18. Harness engineering

Act III. The failure that forced the layer: even a well-fed model acts unreliably — so trust moved from the model to the runtime.

#### 18.1 Definition and origin

A harness is the deterministic runtime layer wrapped around an LLM: it validates schemas, enforces permissions, executes tool calls, tracks budgets, and logs everything, on the principle that the model proposes and the harness disposes. The metaphor is horse tack — reins, saddle, bit: equipment for channeling a powerful but underdirected animal.

The discipline’s current name was popularised in February 2026 by two closely spaced posts. Mitchell Hashimoto’s My AI Adoption Journey (February 5) made "Engineer the Harness" the fifth step of his adoption ladder: when an agent makes a mistake, engineer the environment — AGENTS.md rules, verification tools — so that class of mistake cannot recur, rather than fixing the output by hand (Hashimoto, 2026). Six days later (February 11), OpenAI published Harness engineering: leveraging Codex in an agent-first world (Lopopolo, 2026), the discipline’s anchor case study, whose summary line became its motto: "Agents aren’t hard; the harness is hard." --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
